  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low and cold front move through the region\ntonight into Sunday with rain showers likely.\n\nHigh pressure shifts away from the region late tonight as a low\norganizes and develops over the Midwest. The warm front\nassociated with this low will move northward into the region\nearly Sunday afternoon, but will be quickly followed by a cold \nfront Sunday evening. \n\nRegardless of the exact evolution of the warm front, the leading\nedge of a large area of rain will move into the region by late \nthis evening in response to modest mid and upper level synoptic \nscale lift associate with the cyclogensis. Locations north of\nPhiladelphia will have the greatest chance for a prolonged\nperiod of rainfall tonight into Sunday.\n\nAn easterly flow will be in place this evening through tonight,\nbecoming southeasterly by Sunday morning. As such, low level \nmoisture will be on the increase, with surface dew points rising\nwell into the 60s and precipitable water values increasing to \nnear 2 inches. Precipitable water values that high are well \nabove the 90th percentile. \n\nThe risk for excessive rainfall is a marginal, or 1 out of 4, \nthrough this period. However, with abundant moisture, locally \nheavy rain will still be possible, and if it occurs over urban \nand other poor drainage areas, localized flash flooding is \npossible.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stationary front south of the region could keep \nrain chances around, especially for Delmarva through mid week.\n\nA cold front will track through the forecast area on Sunday night. \nThe front will be south of the region on Monday and remain there \nthrough mid week. Confidence is not the highest pertaining to all \naspects of sensible weather, namely precipitation. How far south \nthat front sags south will be a big factor in how much rain we see \nthrough the week. The further south it stalls, the less likely our \nregion will see continued rain chances through the mid week period. \nRegardless, our region will be on the cool side of the front through \nthis period keeping temperatures below normal. Confidence is higher \nhere. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A closed mid-level low ejecting from the Ohio Valley \ncould help to spin up surface low pressure off our coast by \nThursday, potentially bringing another chance for rain, and \nincreasing the risk for coastal flooding.\n\nAs a mid level low digs in from the northwest, models are showing \nsurface cyclogensis by Wednesday off the Carolinas. At this point, \nthere are significant differences in the track and strength of the \nlow. The closer the track is to the coast, the more likely our \nregion will see rain and gusty winds on Thursday and Friday. An eye \nwill also need to be kept on tides. Track, strength of the low, \nduration of an onshore wind component, and fetch will all play a \nrole with tidal departures. In addition, the full moon is on \nSaturday. Long story short, some sort of tidal flooding may effect \nthe region next week.",
